// CryptoKit.fls - protocol-level building blocks over the VM's Hash and Crypto natives.
// Version: 1.0
//
// The VM ships the primitives: SHA-1/256/512 and BLAKE2 digests, HMAC, Ed25519,
// X25519, XChaCha20-Poly1305, Argon2id and a CSPRNG. What it does not ship is
// the protocol layer that applications actually call. This library is that
// layer, written entirely on top of the natives, plus a streaming digest engine
// for the cases a one-shot native cannot cover.
//
//   Kdf       HKDF (RFC 5869) and PBKDF2 (RFC 8018) over HMAC-SHA1/256/512.
//   Otp       HOTP (RFC 4226) and TOTP (RFC 6238), Base32 secrets and
//             otpauth:// provisioning URIs for authenticator apps.
//   Password  Argon2id password hashing in the PHC string format
//             ($argon2id$v=19$m=..,t=..,p=1$salt$hash) with Verify/NeedsRehash.
//   Envelope  Authenticated encryption as one self-describing blob
//             (XChaCha20-Poly1305), keyed by a 32-byte key or by a password.
//   Digest    Streaming MD5 / SHA-1 / SHA-256 / SHA-384 / SHA-512 (Update/Final)
//             and one-shot MD5 for legacy interop (S3 ETags, Content-MD5, HTTP
//             Digest auth).
//
// Usage:
//   import { Kdf, Otp, Password, Envelope, Digest } from library("CryptoKit", "1.0");
//
//   let key  = Kdf.Hkdf(shared, salt, "app v1 enc", 32);      // block(32)
//   let dk   = Kdf.Pbkdf2("password", salt, 600000, 32);      // block(32)
//   let code = Otp.Totp(secret);                              // "492039"
//   let ok   = Otp.TotpVerify(secret, userInput);             // bool, +/-1 step
//   let phc  = Password.Hash("hunter2");                      // "$argon2id$v=19$..."
//   let good = Password.Verify("hunter2", phc);               // bool
//   let blob = Envelope.Seal("secret text", key);             // block
//   let back = Envelope.Open(blob, key);                      // block | nil
//   let etag = Digest.Md5(fileBytes);                         // 32-hex
//   let d = new Digest("sha256"); d.Update(a); d.Update(b); let hex = d.Final();
//
// Conventions:
//   - Byte inputs are a string or a 1-byte-element block (Buffer.FromString,
//     Buffer.Create(n, 1), Crypto.RandomBytes, Buffer.Slice). A string is read
//     as its raw bytes and may hold any byte value, including NUL.
//   - Derived keys, raw digests and sealed blobs come back as blocks; text
//     digests come back as lowercase hex.
//   - Bad input returns nil (false from a verifier). Nothing here raises -
//     except where noted: a class method whose parameter has an explicit
//     type (":int" etc.) raises an uncatchable native exception if the
//     caller passes a value of a different type, instead of the compile-time
//     error a free function with the same annotation gets. Every place that
//     matters leaves the parameter untyped and checks Type.Of() itself.
//   - Hash selectors are "sha1", "sha256" or "sha512" (case-insensitive).
//     Kdf defaults to sha256. Otp defaults to sha1, which is what RFC 6238 and
//     every authenticator app assume.
//   - Every comparison of a secret-derived value goes through
//     Crypto.ConstantTimeEquals.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Kdf
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Kdf.HkdfExtract(salt, ikm, hash?)            -> block (PRK, HashLen bytes)
//   Kdf.HkdfExpand(prk, info, length, hash?)     -> block (length bytes) | nil
//   Kdf.Hkdf(ikm, salt, info, length, hash?)     -> block (length bytes) | nil
//   Kdf.Pbkdf2(password, salt, iterations, length, hash?) -> block | nil
//
//   HKDF turns a shared secret (for example Crypto.X25519Shared, which the VM
//   hands you as raw hex) into as many independent keys as you need:
//
//     let ss   = Kdf.HexToBytes(Crypto.X25519Shared(mySk, peerPk));
//     let encK = Kdf.Hkdf(ss, nil, "chat v1 encrypt", 32);
//     let macK = Kdf.Hkdf(ss, nil, "chat v1 mac", 32);
//
//   salt may be nil or empty (HKDF then uses HashLen zero bytes, per the RFC);
//   info may be nil or empty. length is 1..255*HashLen.
//
//   PBKDF2 exists for interoperability with systems that already store PBKDF2
//   hashes. For new password storage use Password (Argon2id). Cost is one
//   native HMAC per iteration: 600 000 iterations of HMAC-SHA256 take about
//   half a second.
//
//   Kdf.HexToBytes(hex) -> block | nil and Kdf.BytesToHex(block) -> string are
//   exported because every native digest and MAC speaks hex while every key
//   consumer wants bytes.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Otp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Otp.Hotp(secret, counter, digits?, hash?)               -> string | nil
//   Otp.Totp(secret, time?, step?, digits?, hash?)          -> string | nil
//   Otp.TotpVerify(secret, code, window?, time?, step?, digits?, hash?) -> bool
//   Otp.NewSecret(bytes?)                                   -> string (Base32)
//   Otp.SecretFromBase32(text)                              -> block | nil
//   Otp.ProvisioningUri(issuer, account, secretBase32, opts?) -> string
//
//   secret is the RAW key bytes (string or block). Authenticator apps show the
//   key as Base32; convert with SecretFromBase32 first. digits is 6..8
//   (default 6), step is the period in seconds (default 30), time is a Unix
//   timestamp (default Time.Now()). TotpVerify accepts codes from the window
//   steps before and after the current one (default 1, i.e. three codes) and
//   compares in constant time. A code of the wrong length or with a non-digit
//   is rejected before any HMAC runs.
//
//   Enrolment:
//     let b32 = Otp.NewSecret();                          // store this
//     let uri = Otp.ProvisioningUri("Acme", "sam@acme.io", b32);
//     // hand `uri` to QRCode.fls; the app scans it
//   Login:
//     let ok = Otp.TotpVerify(Otp.SecretFromBase32(b32), userInput);
//
//   ProvisioningUri opts: { digits, step, hash }. The URI follows the
//   otpauth:// key-URI format that Google Authenticator, Authy, 1Password and
//   the rest read; issuer and account are percent-encoded.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Password
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Password.Hash(password, opts?)        -> string (PHC) | nil
//   Password.Verify(password, phc)        -> bool
//   Password.NeedsRehash(phc, opts?)      -> bool
//   Password.Parse(phc)                   -> object | nil
//
//   Hash draws a 16-byte salt from the CSPRNG, runs Argon2id and returns the
//   PHC string every other Argon2 implementation reads and writes:
//
//     $argon2id$v=19$m=65536,t=3,p=1$<salt b64>$<hash b64>
//
//   opts: { memKiB (default 65536 = 64 MiB), iterations (default 3) }. The VM
//   runs Argon2id with one lane, so p is always 1 and a PHC string with p != 1
//   cannot be verified here (Verify returns false). Tag length is 32 bytes.
//   NeedsRehash is true when the stored parameters are below opts (or the
//   defaults), or the string does not parse - call it after a successful login
//   and re-Hash when it says so. Parse returns { memKiB, iterations,
//   parallelism, version, salt:block, hash:block }.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Envelope
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Envelope.NewKey()                                 -> block (32 random bytes)
//   Envelope.Seal(plain, key)                         -> block | nil
//   Envelope.Open(sealed, key)                        -> block | nil
//   Envelope.SealWithPassword(plain, password, opts?) -> block | nil
//   Envelope.OpenWithPassword(sealed, password)       -> block | nil
//   Envelope.DeriveKey(password, salt, opts?)         -> block (32) | nil
//
//   Crypto.Encrypt hands back cipher, nonce and tag as three values. Seal packs
//   them into one blob you can write to a file, a database column or a wire:
//
//     v1 (key):      [0x01][nonce 24][tag 16][ciphertext]
//     v2 (password): [0x02][memKiB u32 BE][iterations u8][salt 16][nonce 24][tag 16][ciphertext]
//
//   Open verifies the tag before returning anything; a wrong key, a wrong
//   password, a flipped bit or a truncated blob all give nil. plain must be at
//   least one byte (the native AEAD rejects an empty message). The password
//   forms derive the key with Argon2id (opts as for Password.Hash) and record
//   the parameters in the header, so a blob stays readable after you raise the
//   defaults. Open returns bytes; Buffer.ToString(bytes) gives the text back.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Digest
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Digest.Md5(data, raw?)              -> string (32-hex) | block (16) | nil
//   Digest.Keccak256(data, raw?)        -> string (64-hex) | block (32) | nil
//   Digest.Shake128(data, outLen, raw?) -> string (2*outLen hex) | block (outLen) | nil
//   Digest.Shake256(data, outLen, raw?) -> string (2*outLen hex) | block (outLen) | nil
//   Digest.Algorithms()                 -> ["md5", "sha1", "sha256", "sha384", "sha512",
//                                            "sha3-224", "sha3-256", "sha3-384", "sha3-512",
//                                            "keccak256", "shake128", "shake256"]
//   new Digest(algo)                    -> streaming hasher
//     .Update(data)                     -> this (chainable); nil after Final/Squeeze
//     .Final(raw?)                      -> string hex | block; idempotent; nil for SHAKE
//     .Squeeze(outLen, raw?)            -> string hex | block; SHAKE128/256 only, one-shot
//     .Reset()                          -> this
//     .Algorithm()                      -> "md5" | "sha1" | ... | "shake256"
//
//   The native digests are one-shot: the whole input must be in memory. The
//   streaming form hashes a download, an upload or a socket stream chunk by
//   chunk with a state no bigger than one block. MD5 is provided here, and
//   only here, for protocols that still require it; it is not a security
//   primitive and the VM deliberately does not offer it. Every compression
//   function is a typed leaf the JIT compiles to native code: MD5 runs at
//   about three quarters of the native SHA-256's speed, SHA-1 and SHA-256 at
//   about a third, SHA-384/512 similarly (the wider block amortizes better).
//   SHA-3/Keccak/SHAKE run a from-scratch, non-unrolled Keccak-f[1600] and
//   are about ten times slower than native SHA-256 - correct, but not the
//   algorithm to reach for when only speed matters and SHA-2 is acceptable.
//
//   SHA-384/512 use 64-bit words and the `>>>` operator; the whole Keccak
//   family additionally stores its 25-lane state in a flat `[int]` array
//   outside the tagged +-2^60 range. Both need VM 1.0.3.0 or later - a JIT
//   bug before that release corrupted such values round-tripped through a
//   typed `[int]` array. SHA-384 is SHA-512 with different starting values
//   and the output cut to the first 48 bytes.
//
//   Keccak-256 is the pre-standardization Keccak, still what "keccak256"
//   means in Ethereum and most blockchain tooling: the exact same
//   permutation as SHA3-256, differing only in one padding byte (0x01
//   instead of NIST's 0x06 domain separator), which makes it a genuinely
//   different 32-byte hash, not an alias - do not use it expecting SHA3-256,
//   or vice versa. SHAKE128/256 are extendable-output functions: pick any
//   output length with Squeeze, which (unlike Final) can only be called once
//   per instance - Reset() first for a different length.
//
// ===========================================================================

import { Util } from library("Util", "1.0");

const M32 = 0xFFFFFFFF;

const HASH_SHA1   = 1;
const HASH_SHA256 = 2;
const HASH_SHA512 = 3;

const DIGEST_MD5    = 1;
const DIGEST_SHA1   = 2;
const DIGEST_SHA256 = 3;
const DIGEST_SHA384 = 4;
const DIGEST_SHA512 = 5;
const DIGEST_SHA3_224 = 6;
const DIGEST_SHA3_256 = 7;
const DIGEST_SHA3_384 = 8;
const DIGEST_SHA3_512 = 9;
const DIGEST_KECCAK256 = 10;
const DIGEST_SHAKE128 = 11;
const DIGEST_SHAKE256 = 12;

const ARGON_DEFAULT_MEM  = 65536;   // KiB (64 MiB), the VM's own default
const ARGON_DEFAULT_ITER = 3;
const ARGON_MIN_MEM      = 8;       // Argon2 requires >= 8*p KiB
const ARGON_MAX_MEM      = 1048576; // the VM caps at 1 GiB
const ARGON_SALT_BYTES   = 16;

const ENVELOPE_V1 = 1;              // key-sealed
const ENVELOPE_V2 = 2;              // password-sealed
const NONCE_BYTES = 24;
const TAG_BYTES   = 16;
const KEY_BYTES   = 32;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Shared helpers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// "sha1" | "sha256" | "sha512" (any case) -> HASH_*; nil -> dflt; unknown -> 0.
fn _hashId(name, dflt: int): int {
    if (name == nil) { return dflt; }
    if (Type.Of(name) != Type.String) { return 0; }
    let n = String.ToLower(name);
    if (n == "sha256" || n == "sha-256") { return HASH_SHA256; }
    if (n == "sha1"   || n == "sha-1")   { return HASH_SHA1; }
    if (n == "sha512" || n == "sha-512") { return HASH_SHA512; }
    return 0;
}

fn _hashName(id: int): string {
    if (id == HASH_SHA256) { return "SHA256"; }
    if (id == HASH_SHA512) { return "SHA512"; }
    return "SHA1";
}

fn _hashLen(id: int): int {
    if (id == HASH_SHA256) { return 32; }
    if (id == HASH_SHA512) { return 64; }
    return 20;
}

// HMAC over the selected hash; key and data are string|block. Hex out.
fn _hmacHex(id: int, key, data): string {
    if (id == HASH_SHA256) { return Crypto.HmacSha256(key, data); }
    if (id == HASH_SHA512) { return Crypto.HmacSha512(key, data); }
    return Crypto.HmacSha1(key, data);
}

// True for the byte-carrying types this library accepts.
fn _isBytes(x): bool {
    let t = Type.Of(x);
    return t == Type.String || t == Type.Block;
}

// Byte length of a string|block; 0 for nil.
fn _byteLen(x): int {
    if (x == nil) { return 0; }
    return len(x);
}

// string|block -> block. Strings are copied; blocks pass through. Empty or
// nil gives nil because the VM has no zero-length block.
fn _toBlock(x): block {
    if (x == nil) { return nil; }
    if (Type.Of(x) == Type.Block) { return len(x) > 0 ? x : nil; }
    if (Type.Of(x) != Type.String || len(x) == 0) { return nil; }
    return Buffer.FromString(x);
}

// Decode 2*n lowercase/uppercase hex chars of `hex` into out[0..n).
// A typed leaf: string, block and int parameters, no allocation.
fn _hexInto(hex: string, out: block, n: int) {
    let k: int = 0;
    let j: int = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        let c1: int = hex[j] & 255;
        let c2: int = hex[j + 1] & 255;
        let hi: int = c1 > 57 ? ((c1 | 32) - 87) : (c1 - 48);
        let lo: int = c2 > 57 ? ((c2 | 32) - 87) : (c2 - 48);
        out[k] = (hi << 4) | lo;
        k = k + 1;
        j = j + 2;
    }
}

fn _hexToBlock(hex): block {
    if (hex == nil || Type.Of(hex) != Type.String) { return nil; }
    let n = len(hex);
    if (n == 0 || (n & 1) != 0) { return nil; }
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        if (!Char.IsXDigit(hex[i])) { return nil; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let out = Buffer.Create(n / 2, 1);
    _hexInto(hex, out, n / 2);
    return out;
}

// Unpadded Base64 (PHC strings use it).
fn _b64NoPad(b: block): string {
    let s = Util.Base64Encode(b);
    let n = len(s);
    while (n > 0 && s[n - 1] == '=') { n = n - 1; }
    return String.Substr(s, 0, n);
}

fn _b64Decode(s: string): block {
    if (s == nil || len(s) == 0) { return nil; }
    let padded = s;
    while ((len(padded) & 3) != 0) { padded = padded + "="; }
    return Util.Base64DecodeBlock(padded);
}

// Parse an unsigned decimal; nil on anything else.
fn _parseUInt(s) {
    if (s == nil || Type.Of(s) != Type.String || len(s) == 0 || len(s) > 12) { return nil; }
    let i = 0;
    let v = 0;
    while (i < len(s)) {
        if (!Char.IsDigit(s[i])) { return nil; }
        v = v * 10 + (s[i] - '0');
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// An optional int option: dflt when absent, nil when present but not an int.
fn _optInt(v, dflt: int) {
    if (v == nil) { return dflt; }
    if (Type.Of(v) != Type.Int) { return nil; }
    return v;
}

fn _argonParams(opts) {
    let m = _optInt(opts == nil ? nil : opts.memKiB, ARGON_DEFAULT_MEM);
    let t = _optInt(opts == nil ? nil : opts.iterations, ARGON_DEFAULT_ITER);
    if (m == nil || t == nil) { return nil; }
    if (m < ARGON_MIN_MEM || m > ARGON_MAX_MEM || t < 1 || t > 255) { return nil; }
    return { memKiB: m, iterations: t };
}

// RFC 3986 percent-encoding: unreserved bytes pass, everything else is %XX.
fn _pctEncode(s: string): string {
    let out = "";
    let i = 0;
    while (i < len(s)) {
        let c = s[i];
        if (Char.IsAlNum(c) || c == '-' || c == '.' || c == '_' || c == '~') {
            out = out + c;
        } else {
            let piece = "%" + String.Format("{0:X2}", c & 255);
            out = out + piece;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Kdf
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The PBKDF2 inner loop, one leaf per hash so that the HMAC call is the
// initializer of a local (the form the JIT lowers). Each round feeds the
// previous MAC back in and XORs it into t; u and t are HashLen blocks.
fn _xorHexInto(h: string, u: block, t: block, hlen: int) {
    let k: int = 0;
    let j: int = 0;
    while (k < hlen) {
        let c1: int = h[j] & 255;
        let c2: int = h[j + 1] & 255;
        let hi: int = c1 > 57 ? ((c1 | 32) - 87) : (c1 - 48);
        let lo: int = c2 > 57 ? ((c2 | 32) - 87) : (c2 - 48);
        let v: int = (hi << 4) | lo;
        u[k] = v;
        t[k] = (t[k] ^ v) & 255;
        k = k + 1;
        j = j + 2;
    }
}

fn _pbkdf2RoundsSha1(pw: string, u: block, t: block, rounds: int) {
    let r: int = 0;
    while (r < rounds) {
        let h: string = Crypto.HmacSha1(pw, u);
        _xorHexInto(h, u, t, 20);
        r = r + 1;
    }
}

fn _pbkdf2RoundsSha256(pw: string, u: block, t: block, rounds: int) {
    let r: int = 0;
    while (r < rounds) {
        let h: string = Crypto.HmacSha256(pw, u);
        _xorHexInto(h, u, t, 32);
        r = r + 1;
    }
}

fn _pbkdf2RoundsSha512(pw: string, u: block, t: block, rounds: int) {
    let r: int = 0;
    while (r < rounds) {
        let h: string = Crypto.HmacSha512(pw, u);
        _xorHexInto(h, u, t, 64);
        r = r + 1;
    }
}

class Kdf {

    fn static HexToBytes(hex): block { return _hexToBlock(hex); }

    fn static BytesToHex(b): string {
        if (b == nil || Type.Of(b) != Type.Block) { return nil; }
        return Util.BinToHex(b);
    }

    // HKDF-Extract: PRK = HMAC(salt, IKM). Empty/nil salt = HashLen zero bytes.
    fn static HkdfExtract(salt, ikm, hash?): block {
        let id = _hashId(hash, HASH_SHA256);
        if (id == 0 || !_isBytes(ikm) || len(ikm) == 0) { return nil; }
        let key = salt;
        if (_byteLen(salt) == 0) {
            key = Buffer.Create(_hashLen(id), 1);
            Buffer.Fill(key, 0);
        } else if (!_isBytes(salt)) {
            return nil;
        }
        return _hexToBlock(_hmacHex(id, key, ikm));
    }

    // HKDF-Expand: OKM = T(1) | T(2) | ...  with T(i) = HMAC(PRK, T(i-1) | info | i).
    fn static HkdfExpand(prk, info, length: int, hash?): block {
        let id = _hashId(hash, HASH_SHA256);
        if (id == 0 || !_isBytes(prk) || len(prk) == 0) { return nil; }
        let hlen = _hashLen(id);
        if (length < 1 || length > 255 * hlen) { return nil; }
        let infoB = nil;
        if (info != nil) {
            if (!_isBytes(info)) { return nil; }
            infoB = _toBlock(info);
        }
        let infoLen = _byteLen(infoB);

        let okm = Buffer.Create(length, 1);
        let written = 0;
        let prev = nil;
        let i = 1;
        while (written < length) {
            let prevLen = (prev == nil) ? 0 : hlen;
            let msg = Buffer.Create(prevLen + infoLen + 1, 1);
            if (prevLen > 0) { Buffer.Copy(prev, 0, msg, 0, hlen); }
            if (infoLen > 0) { Buffer.Copy(infoB, 0, msg, prevLen, infoLen); }
            msg[prevLen + infoLen] = i;
            let t = _hexToBlock(_hmacHex(id, prk, msg));
            let take = length - written;
            if (take > hlen) { take = hlen; }
            Buffer.Copy(t, 0, okm, written, take);
            written = written + take;
            prev = t;
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return okm;
    }

    fn static Hkdf(ikm, salt, info, length: int, hash?): block {
        let prk = Kdf.HkdfExtract(salt, ikm, hash);
        if (prk == nil) { return nil; }
        return Kdf.HkdfExpand(prk, info, length, hash);
    }

    // PBKDF2: DK = T(1) | T(2) | ...  with T(i) = U1 ^ U2 ^ ... ^ Uc,
    // U1 = HMAC(P, S | INT32BE(i)), Uj = HMAC(P, Uj-1).
    fn static Pbkdf2(password, salt, iterations: int, length: int, hash?): block {
        let id = _hashId(hash, HASH_SHA256);
        if (id == 0 || !_isBytes(password) || !_isBytes(salt)) { return nil; }
        if (iterations < 1 || length < 1) { return nil; }
        let hlen = _hashLen(id);
        if (length > 4294967295 * hlen) { return nil; }

        // The typed leaf takes the password as a string so it stays JIT-eligible;
        // a block is re-read as bytes (NUL survives).
        let pw = (Type.Of(password) == Type.String) ? password : Buffer.ToString(password);
        let saltLen = len(salt);
        let saltB = _toBlock(salt);

        let dk = Buffer.Create(length, 1);
        let u = Buffer.Create(hlen, 1);
        let t = Buffer.Create(hlen, 1);
        let s1 = Buffer.Create(saltLen + 4, 1);
        if (saltLen > 0) { Buffer.Copy(saltB, 0, s1, 0, saltLen); }

        let written = 0;
        let i = 1;
        while (written < length) {
            Buffer.WriteU32At(s1, saltLen, i, true);
            _hexInto(_hmacHex(id, pw, s1), u, hlen);
            Buffer.Copy(u, 0, t, 0, hlen);
            if (id == HASH_SHA256)      { _pbkdf2RoundsSha256(pw, u, t, iterations - 1); }
            else if (id == HASH_SHA512) { _pbkdf2RoundsSha512(pw, u, t, iterations - 1); }
            else                        { _pbkdf2RoundsSha1(pw, u, t, iterations - 1); }
            let take = length - written;
            if (take > hlen) { take = hlen; }
            Buffer.Copy(t, 0, dk, written, take);
            written = written + take;
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return dk;
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Otp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Otp {

    // HOTP(K, C): dynamic truncation of HMAC(K, C as 8-byte big-endian).
    fn static Hotp(secret, counter: int, digits?, hash?): string {
        let id = _hashId(hash, HASH_SHA1);
        let d = (digits == nil) ? 6 : digits;
        if (id == 0 || Type.Of(d) != Type.Int || d < 6 || d > 8) { return nil; }
        if (!_isBytes(secret) || len(secret) == 0 || counter < 0) { return nil; }

        let msg = Buffer.Create(8, 1);
        Buffer.WriteU64At(msg, 0, counter, true);
        let mac = _hexToBlock(_hmacHex(id, secret, msg));
        let n = len(mac);
        let off = mac[n - 1] & 15;
        let bin = ((mac[off] & 127) << 24)
                | ((mac[off + 1] & 255) << 16)
                | ((mac[off + 2] & 255) << 8)
                |  (mac[off + 3] & 255);
        let code = bin % (10 ^^ d);
        return String.PadLeft(str(code), d, '0');
    }

    // TOTP = HOTP(K, floor(time / step)).
    fn static Totp(secret, time?, step?, digits?, hash?): string {
        let t = (time == nil) ? Time.Now() : time;
        let s = (step == nil) ? 30 : step;
        if (Type.Of(t) != Type.Int || Type.Of(s) != Type.Int || s < 1 || t < 0) { return nil; }
        return Otp.Hotp(secret, t / s, digits, hash);
    }

    // True when `code` matches any step in [now - window, now + window].
    fn static TotpVerify(secret, code, window?, time?, step?, digits?, hash?): bool {
        let w = (window == nil) ? 1 : window;
        let d = (digits == nil) ? 6 : digits;
        let t = (time == nil) ? Time.Now() : time;
        let s = (step == nil) ? 30 : step;
        if (Type.Of(w) != Type.Int || w < 0 || w > 100) { return false; }
        if (Type.Of(d) != Type.Int || Type.Of(t) != Type.Int || Type.Of(s) != Type.Int) { return false; }
        if (s < 1 || t < 0) { return false; }
        if (code == nil || Type.Of(code) != Type.String || len(code) != d) { return false; }
        let i = 0;
        while (i < d) {
            if (!Char.IsDigit(code[i])) { return false; }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        let counter = t / s;
        let ok = false;
        let k = 0 - w;
        while (k <= w) {
            let c = counter + k;
            if (c >= 0) {
                let expect = Otp.Hotp(secret, c, d, hash);
                if (expect != nil && Crypto.ConstantTimeEquals(expect, code)) { ok = true; }
            }
            k = k + 1;
        }
        return ok;
    }

    // A fresh random secret as unpadded Base32 (20 bytes = 160 bits by default,
    // which is what RFC 4226 recommends and what authenticator apps expect).
    fn static NewSecret(bytes?): string {
        let n = (bytes == nil) ? 20 : bytes;
        if (Type.Of(n) != Type.Int || n < 10 || n > 64) { return nil; }
        let s = Util.Base32Encode(Crypto.RandomBytes(n));
        let m = len(s);
        while (m > 0 && s[m - 1] == '=') { m = m - 1; }
        return String.Substr(s, 0, m);
    }

    // Base32 text as shown to the user (any case, spaces and dashes allowed,
    // padding optional) -> raw key bytes.
    fn static SecretFromBase32(text): block {
        if (text == nil || Type.Of(text) != Type.String) { return nil; }
        let clean = "";
        let i = 0;
        while (i < len(text)) {
            let c = text[i];
            if (c != ' ' && c != '-' && c != '=') {
                clean = clean + Char.ToUpper(c);
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (len(clean) == 0) { return nil; }
        while ((len(clean) & 7) != 0) { clean = clean + "="; }
        return Util.Base32DecodeBlock(clean);
    }

    // otpauth://totp/<issuer>:<account>?secret=..&issuer=..&algorithm=..&digits=..&period=..
    fn static ProvisioningUri(issuer, account, secretBase32, opts?): string {
        if (issuer == nil || account == nil || secretBase32 == nil) { return nil; }
        if (Type.Of(issuer) != Type.String || Type.Of(account) != Type.String) { return nil; }
        if (Type.Of(secretBase32) != Type.String || len(secretBase32) == 0) { return nil; }
        let d = _optInt(opts == nil ? nil : opts.digits, 6);
        let s = _optInt(opts == nil ? nil : opts.step, 30);
        let hashName = (opts == nil) ? nil : opts.hash;
        let id = _hashId(hashName, HASH_SHA1);
        if (d == nil || s == nil || id == 0 || d < 6 || d > 8 || s < 1) { return nil; }

        let secret = String.Replace(String.ToUpper(secretBase32), "=", "");
        let label = _pctEncode(issuer) + ":" + _pctEncode(account);
        return "otpauth://totp/" + label
             + "?secret=" + secret
             + "&issuer=" + _pctEncode(issuer)
             + "&algorithm=" + _hashName(id)
             + "&digits=" + str(d)
             + "&period=" + str(s);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Password
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// "$argon2id$v=19$m=65536,t=3,p=1$<salt>$<hash>" -> fields, or nil.
// The "v=19" segment is optional (some encoders omit it).
fn _parsePhc(phc) {
    if (phc == nil || Type.Of(phc) != Type.String || len(phc) > 512) { return nil; }
    let parts = String.Split(phc, "$");
    // parts[0] is the empty string before the leading '$'
    if (len(parts) < 5 || parts[0] != "" || parts[1] != "argon2id") { return nil; }
    let idx = 2;
    let version = 19;
    if (String.StartsWith(parts[idx], "v=")) {
        version = _parseUInt(String.Substr(parts[idx], 2));
        if (version == nil) { return nil; }
        idx = idx + 1;
    }
    if (len(parts) != idx + 3) { return nil; }

    let m = nil; let t = nil; let p = nil;
    let kvs = String.Split(parts[idx], ",");
    let i = 0;
    while (i < len(kvs)) {
        let kv = String.Split(kvs[i], "=");
        if (len(kv) != 2) { return nil; }
        let v = _parseUInt(kv[1]);
        if (v == nil) { return nil; }
        if (kv[0] == "m") { m = v; }
        else if (kv[0] == "t") { t = v; }
        else if (kv[0] == "p") { p = v; }
        else { return nil; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (m == nil || t == nil || p == nil) { return nil; }
    let salt = _b64Decode(parts[idx + 1]);
    let hash = _b64Decode(parts[idx + 2]);
    if (salt == nil || hash == nil || len(salt) < 8) { return nil; }
    return { memKiB: m, iterations: t, parallelism: p, version: version, salt: salt, hash: hash };
}

class Password {

    fn static Hash(password, opts?): string {
        if (!_isBytes(password)) { return nil; }
        let a = _argonParams(opts);
        if (a == nil) { return nil; }
        let salt = Crypto.RandomBytes(ARGON_SALT_BYTES);
        let hex = Crypto.Argon2(password, salt, a.iterations, a.memKiB);
        let tag = _hexToBlock(hex);
        if (tag == nil) { return nil; }
        return "$argon2id$v=19$m=" + str(a.memKiB) + ",t=" + str(a.iterations) + ",p=1$"
             + _b64NoPad(salt) + "$" + _b64NoPad(tag);
    }

    fn static Verify(password, phc): bool {
        if (!_isBytes(password)) { return false; }
        let p = _parsePhc(phc);
        if (p == nil) { return false; }
        if (p.version != 19 || p.parallelism != 1 || len(p.hash) != 32) { return false; }
        if (p.memKiB < ARGON_MIN_MEM || p.memKiB > ARGON_MAX_MEM || p.iterations < 1) { return false; }
        let hex = Crypto.Argon2(password, p.salt, p.iterations, p.memKiB);
        let tag = _hexToBlock(hex);
        if (tag == nil) { return false; }
        return Crypto.ConstantTimeEquals(tag, p.hash);
    }

    // True when the stored hash is weaker than opts (or the defaults), or is
    // not one this library can verify.
    fn static NeedsRehash(phc, opts?): bool {
        let a = _argonParams(opts);
        if (a == nil) { return true; }
        let p = _parsePhc(phc);
        if (p == nil) { return true; }
        if (p.version != 19 || p.parallelism != 1 || len(p.hash) != 32) { return true; }
        return p.memKiB < a.memKiB || p.iterations < a.iterations;
    }

    fn static Parse(phc) { return _parsePhc(phc); }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Envelope
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// nonce | tag | cipher appended to `out` at `off`; returns total length.
fn _sealInto(plain, key, out: block, off: int): bool {
    let n = len(plain);
    let r = Crypto.Encrypt(plain, n, key);
    if (!r.Ok) { return false; }
    let tag = Buffer.Create(TAG_BYTES, 1);
    _hexInto(r.Tag, tag, TAG_BYTES);
    Buffer.Copy(r.Nonce, 0, out, off, NONCE_BYTES);
    Buffer.Copy(tag, 0, out, off + NONCE_BYTES, TAG_BYTES);
    Buffer.Copy(r.Cipher, 0, out, off + NONCE_BYTES + TAG_BYTES, n);
    return true;
}

fn _openFrom(sealed: block, key, off: int): block {
    let total = len(sealed);
    let n = total - off - NONCE_BYTES - TAG_BYTES;
    if (n < 1) { return nil; }
    let nonce  = Buffer.Slice(sealed, off, NONCE_BYTES);
    let tag    = Util.BinToHex(Buffer.Slice(sealed, off + NONCE_BYTES, TAG_BYTES));
    let cipher = Buffer.Slice(sealed, off + NONCE_BYTES + TAG_BYTES, n);
    let d = Crypto.Decrypt(cipher, n, key, nonce, tag);
    if (!d.Ok) { return nil; }
    return d.Plain;
}

class Envelope {

    fn static NewKey(): block { return Crypto.RandomBytes(KEY_BYTES); }

    fn static Seal(plain, key): block {
        if (!_isBytes(plain) || len(plain) < 1) { return nil; }
        if (!_isBytes(key) || len(key) != KEY_BYTES) { return nil; }
        let out = Buffer.Create(1 + NONCE_BYTES + TAG_BYTES + len(plain), 1);
        out[0] = ENVELOPE_V1;
        if (!_sealInto(plain, key, out, 1)) { return nil; }
        return out;
    }

    fn static Open(sealed, key): block {
        let b = _toBlock(sealed);
        if (b == nil || !_isBytes(key) || len(key) != KEY_BYTES) { return nil; }
        if (len(b) < 1 + NONCE_BYTES + TAG_BYTES + 1 || b[0] != ENVELOPE_V1) { return nil; }
        return _openFrom(b, key, 1);
    }

    // Argon2id(password, salt) -> 32-byte key. opts as for Password.Hash.
    fn static DeriveKey(password, salt, opts?): block {
        if (!_isBytes(password) || !_isBytes(salt) || len(salt) < 8) { return nil; }
        let a = _argonParams(opts);
        if (a == nil) { return nil; }
        return _hexToBlock(Crypto.Argon2(password, salt, a.iterations, a.memKiB));
    }

    fn static SealWithPassword(plain, password, opts?): block {
        if (!_isBytes(plain) || len(plain) < 1 || !_isBytes(password)) { return nil; }
        let a = _argonParams(opts);
        if (a == nil) { return nil; }
        let salt = Crypto.RandomBytes(ARGON_SALT_BYTES);
        let key = _hexToBlock(Crypto.Argon2(password, salt, a.iterations, a.memKiB));
        if (key == nil) { return nil; }
        let hdr = 1 + 4 + 1 + ARGON_SALT_BYTES;
        let out = Buffer.Create(hdr + NONCE_BYTES + TAG_BYTES + len(plain), 1);
        out[0] = ENVELOPE_V2;
        Buffer.WriteU32At(out, 1, a.memKiB, true);
        out[5] = a.iterations;
        Buffer.Copy(salt, 0, out, 6, ARGON_SALT_BYTES);
        if (!_sealInto(plain, key, out, hdr)) { return nil; }
        return out;
    }

    fn static OpenWithPassword(sealed, password): block {
        let b = _toBlock(sealed);
        if (b == nil || !_isBytes(password)) { return nil; }
        let hdr = 1 + 4 + 1 + ARGON_SALT_BYTES;
        if (len(b) < hdr + NONCE_BYTES + TAG_BYTES + 1 || b[0] != ENVELOPE_V2) { return nil; }
        let m = Buffer.ReadU32At(b, 1, true);
        let t = b[5] & 255;
        if (m < ARGON_MIN_MEM || m > ARGON_MAX_MEM || t < 1) { return nil; }
        let salt = Buffer.Slice(b, 6, ARGON_SALT_BYTES);
        let key = _hexToBlock(Crypto.Argon2(password, salt, t, m));
        if (key == nil) { return nil; }
        return _openFrom(b, key, hdr);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Digest - streaming MD5 / SHA-1 / SHA-256 / SHA-384 / SHA-512 / SHA-3 / Keccak / SHAKE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Two families, both compiled to native code as typed leaves over
// (state, data, offset, block count, scratch):
//
//   Merkle-Damgard (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512): 32-bit words live
//   in 64-bit ints and are masked with M32 after every add or left shift;
//   `x ^ M32` is the 32-bit complement. SHA-384/512 use real 64-bit words
//   (no masking - overflow is the correct behaviour) and `>>>` for the
//   logical right shifts a rotate needs.
//
//   Keccak sponge (SHA3-224/256/384/512, Keccak-256, SHAKE128/256): a 25-lane
//   (5x5x64-bit) state permuted by Keccak-f[1600] (function _keccakF below),
//   absorbing "rate" bytes at a time (_keccakAbsorb) and squeezing the output
//   the same way. The five algorithms share one permutation and differ only
//   in rate, output length, and a one-byte padding constant - see the class
//   Constructor. KECCAK_RC (round constants) and KECCAK_ROT (per-lane rotate
//   amounts, flattened to the same x+5*y index as the state) are the two
//   fixed tables the standard defines; both are reproduced here from a
//   Python reference implementation that was cross-checked byte-for-byte
//   against Python's own hashlib (sha3_224/256/384/512, shake_128/256) and
//   against the EIP-1052 constant for Keccak-256("") before anything below
//   was written from it - see tests/test_cryptokit.fls for the vectors that
//   resulted. Unrolling the rho/pi step to remove its two runtime `%5`
//   operations is the obvious next speedup, not yet done.

